    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72730",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(C)=-46.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{9}:=-\\frac{2}{23}{R}_{9}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{2}{23}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-\\frac{2}{23}det(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{23}\\cdot (-46) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
